---
title: Verify an Alibi
description: Check a purchase's Alibi on your machine with @keelson/verifier, from code or from the command line.
estimate: 3 min
section: SDK
order: 5
---

`@keelson/verifier` checks an Alibi on your machine, trusting no one, with the same code as [verify.keelson.finance](https://verify.keelson.finance): both TEE countersignatures, the evidence and the log inclusion proof. With `chain`, it also reads the checkpoint's anchor and the signer registry on Arbitrum Sepolia.

## From code

{% languages label="Verify from code" %}
```ts
import { verifyAlibi } from "@keelson/verifier";

const report = await verifyAlibi(await keelson.alibis.retrieve(purchaseId));
console.log(report.verdict); // verified-offline
```

```python
from keelson import verify_alibi

report = verify_alibi(keelson.alibis.retrieve(purchase_id))
print(report["verdict"])  # verified-offline
```
{% /languages %}

`verifyAlibi` takes the Alibi as issued (bytes), as base64 or JSON text, or parsed. Its options:

| Option | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `chain: true` | Also reads the anchor on chain, through the public RPCs |
| `chain: { rpcUrl }` | The same, through your RPC |
| `now` | The time to check validity at |

Without `chain`, the verdict is at best `verified-offline`. `fetchAndVerify(purchaseId)` reads a public Alibi from Keelson's API and verifies it here.

The Python `verify_alibi(alibi, chain=False, rpc_url=None)` runs the same verifier through `keelson-verify`, so both languages give the same verdict for the same bytes. It needs Node.js 20 or later. Until the package is on npm, point it at the one you built: `KEELSON_VERIFY_COMMAND="node /path/to/keelson-paypal/packages/verify/dist/cli.js"`.

## From the command line

```sh {% check="sdk-keelson-verify-json" %}
pnpm --filter "@keelson/verifier..." build > /dev/null
node packages/verify/dist/cli.js --json apps/docs/public/samples/01M6ZWZJHWRRHQEYNJAT7NMSV8.alibi 2> /dev/null | jq -r .verdict
# expect: ^verified-offline$
```

`keelson-verify [--chain] [--rpc <url>] [--json] <file | - | purchase id>` reads an Alibi from a file, from standard input (`-`), or from Keelson's API by purchase id. It exits 0 when the Alibi is verified, 1 when a check fails and 2 when the result is incomplete, so a script can act on it.

## What the report holds

| Field | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `verdict` | `verified`, `verified-unanchored`, `verified-offline`, `incomplete` or `failed` |
| `checks` | Each check with its `id`, its `status` (`pass`, `fail`, `pending`, `absent`, `undisclosed` or `unreachable`), the pointers it `reads` and, when it did not pass, a `reason` |
| `mode` | `2of2`, or `degraded` when only one leg countersigned |
| `unbound` | Values no signature or hash covers, shown as the operator's word |
| `ms` | How long the verification took |

The checks are the same ones listed in [Verify an Alibi](/quickstart/verify-an-alibi/), and what each one proves is in the [threat model](/security/threat-model/).
